    {
      "title": "Neckline and collar",
      "slug": "neckline-and-collar",
      "displayOrder": 20,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 253,
        "lessonOrder": 20
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n     Because the collar is a simple fold-over, the \"wrong\" side of your knitting will \r\n              show. <br>\r\n\r\n\r\n       <figure><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/neckline.jpg\"><br><figcaption align=\"center\"> \r\n          <em>In this sample, the purl side of the collar is the public side. The yarn makes it interesting and the crochet edge adds a finishing touch.</em></figcaption>\r\n          </figure>\r\n         \r\n\r\n<hr>\r\n\r\n              \r\n   <h2 class=\"section-title MachineknittingColor\">Suggestions:</h2>      \r\n\r\n \r\n<ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n<li>Knit a reversible or nearly reversible fabric. Use your ribber or a garter carriage<br></li>\r\n<li>Many single bed stitch patterns look great on both sides<br></li>\r\n<li>Hand manipulate the collar section</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<figure><center><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/collar_fold.gif\"></center><figcaption align=\"center\"><br><em>The grey dotted lines indicate the fold for the collar. To avoid the purl side showing, reform stitches in this area.</em></figcaption>\r\n</figure>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4167,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/neck_opening.gif\">\r\n <br><strong style=\"font-size: 20px;\">Adjust the size of the Neck Opening</strong><br>\r\nThe size of the neck opening is controlled by the bind off and cast on edge on the main piece (the \"N\" dimension).\r\n<br><b>To make a smaller neck opening, bind off fewer stitches for \"N\".</b>\r\n\r\n<ul><li> (This has to go over the head ... don't make it too small) </li>\r\n<li>Remember that this will also result in a smaller folded collar.</li></ul>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/Ndimension.gif\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4168,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "A little planning for a beautiful collar",
          "slug": "a-little-planning-for-a-beautiful-collar",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3161,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\">Thanks To Nancy Mangeri for sharing the instructions for her beautiful collar addition to her L'Envelope<br><b>Nancy's Collar</b><br>\r\n<em>Stitch multiple 8 sts x 16 rows</em><br>\r\n<ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n<li>Block the body and rehang the neck stitches. <em>Be sure to pick up a multiple of 8 stitches.</em></li>\r\n\r\n\r\n<li>Transfer stitches to the ribber for 4x4 rib</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Knit 4 rows in ribbing</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Transfer to ALL stitches to main bed and knit 4 rows plain</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Transfer alternate stitches to the ribber for 4x4 rib</li>\r\n<li>Knit 4 rows in ribbing</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Transfer to all stitches to ribber and knit 4 rows plain</li>\r\n<li>Repeat these 16 rows for desired length</li>\r\n<li>Bind off</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\nThe knitting naturally rolls to the knit side of the stockinette / reversed stockinette  forming the ridges and the 4x4 ribbing causes the waves.\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/nancy3.jpg\"></div>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<hr><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/nancy1.jpg\"></div>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/nancy_collar1.jpg\"></div>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/nancy_collar2.jpg\"></div>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4195,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Nancy's Ribbed Collar",
          "slug": "nancy-s-ribbed-collar",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3174,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sleeve",
      "slug": "sleeve",
      "displayOrder": 21,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 262,
        "lessonOrder": 21
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "The \"sleeve\" is added to the edge of the main garment piece - joined and knitted as you go, from top to hem. No seaming!<br>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\">\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/fit_slideshow7.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\">\r\n<div class='embed-video-container'>\n<iframe src='https://player.vimeo.com/video/297505303' frameborder='0' webkitAllowFullScreen mozallowfullscreen allowFullScreen></iframe>\n</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n<br><hr>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4177,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o fa-2x\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> Long-Time hand knitter, Cindy looked at the pick up for the sleeve a different way than what was suggested in the video above. Here's her suggestion:<br>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\">\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Hang the body stitches on the machine</li>\r\n<li>Knit 1 row</li>\r\n<li>Carriage on the left</li>\r\n<lI>Place the center needles in working position (as directed in the pattern)  <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/1.jpg\"> AND put the remaining needles (opposite the carriage) <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/2.jpg\">  in hold </li>\r\n<li>Knit across the needles in work <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/3.jpg\"></li>\r\n<li>Wrap</li>\r\n<li>Your carriage is on the right and the working yarn is in the middle of the work</li>\r\n<li>Put the left section of needles in hold <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/3.jpg\"> </li>\r\n<li>Continue knitting over the center section of needles <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/1.jpg\">, working short row increases to form the sleeve</li>\r\n<li>Don't forget to wrap!</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"col-12-xs\"><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/alternate_sleeve.jpg\"></div>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4191,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "Sew as  you go",
          "slug": "sew-as-you-go",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3166,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Techniques",
      "slug": "techniques",
      "displayOrder": 40,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 258,
        "lessonOrder": 40
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n    \r\n        <b><em>\"Increase 3 stitches every other row ...\"<br>\r\n        \"Decrease 3 stitches every other row ...\"\r\n        </em></b><br>The pattern is written this way... but by shaping the diagonal edges by casting on and binding off stitches, you'll get the dreaded \"stairsteps\" (plus the edge will be sloppy).\r\n        <br><i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> By using short row shaping, the edge will be picture perfect and more stable.<br><figure>\r\n        <img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/stairsteps1.jpg\">\r\n            <figcaption align=\"center\"><br><em>Top edge is e-wrap cast on stairsteps<br>lower edge is worked with short row shaping and bind off.<br></em></figcaption>\r\n        </figure>\r\n        <br>\r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n    <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/co_bo_pattern.png\">\r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n    \r\n    \r\n        <h2 class=\"section-title MachineknittingColor\"><i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\"></i> The Secret?</h2>\r\n        <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/36/new_concept.gif\">\r\n    \r\n    \r\n    \r\n    <ol>\r\n    <li>\r\n    Read ahead and determine the total number of stitches needed for your cast on.</li>\r\n    <li>With waste yarn and ravel cord, cast on the total number of stitches</li>\r\n    <li>Work a fairly loose closed cast on with the main yarn (e-wrap works great)</li>\r\n    <li>Work short row increases as instructed in the pattern</li>\r\n    <li>Continue following the pattern</li>\r\n    <li>Work short row decreases to complete the section</li>\r\n    <li>Scrap off or bind off loosely</li>\r\n    </ol>\r\n    \r\n    \r\n    \r\n    \r\n\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<h2 class=\"section-title MachineknittingColor\"><i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\"></i> Watch how it's done</h2>\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4152,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151860040",
              "title": "Short  Row Increases and Decreases",
              "legacySlot": 1,
              "legacyComponentId": 4151,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151857333",
              "title": "Automatic Wrap vs Manual Wrap",
              "legacySlot": 2,
              "legacyComponentId": 4151,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<h2 class=\"section-title MachineknittingColor\"><i class=\"fa fa-refresh\"></i> Try this yourself</h2>\r\n\r\n<div>\r\n<h3>Practice</h3>\r\n<ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n      <li>With waste yarn and ravel cord, cast on 30 stitches</li>\r\n       <li>Work a fairly loose closed cast on with the main yarn (e-wrap works great)</li>\r\n        <li><b>All needles in hold</b></li>\r\n       <li><b>Carriage set to hold</b></li>\r\n        <li>\"Cast on(increase) 3 stitches every other row\"<br><ul class=\"instruction-nolist\">\r\n                   <li>Opposite the carriage, put 3 needles into working position</li>\r\n                    <li>Knit across the needles in work</li>\r\n                   <li>Wrap</li>\r\n                     <li>Knit back</li>\r\n                   <li>Repeat until all needles are in work</li>\r\n                   </ul>\r\n  </li>\r\n<li>Knit 10 rows</li>\r\n<li>\"Bind off (decrease) 3 stitches every other row\"<br><ul class=\"instruction-nolist\">\r\n     <li>Opposite the carriage, put 3 needles into hold position</li>\r\n     <li>Knit across the needles in work</li>\r\n     <li>Wrap</li>\r\n     <li>Knit back</li>\r\n     <li>Repeat until all needles are in out of work position</li></ul>\r\n\r\n</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Scrap off or bind off loosely</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4153,
              "order": 3
            }
          ],
